If myths are reading the sky as a living symbolic tableau, then a messiah would not have to be a single permanent star. It could be:

* a wandering star/planet entering a constellation
* a temporary conjunction that changes the figure’s meaning
* a nova/comet/bright transient object that “adds” a new feature
* a seasonal alignment that makes an old figure appear transformed

So yes — a star or planet moving into position so that a constellation suddenly seems to gain a crown, scepter, sword, child, wound, halo, throne, etc. would be exactly the kind of thing ancient sky-symbolic thinking could mythologize.

In that framing, the “messiah” could be:

1. The added sign

Not the base constellation, but the new light that completes it.

Example idea:

* existing constellation = king, virgin, shepherd, lion, man, etc.
* incoming bright object = crown, star on brow, child in arms, heart, wound, or divine mark
* mythic reading = “the anointed one has appeared”

So the messiah becomes the activating element that turns a static figure into a fulfilled one.

2. The fulfiller of a pattern

The constellation may already represent a latent form or prophecy, and the moving star is what fulfills it.

That’s important symbolically:

* the sky already contains the promise
* the new alignment is the completion
* so the messiah is the moment prophecy becomes visible

That’s a very mythic structure.

3. A temporary enthronement

A crown is especially interesting, because a crown is not the person — it is the sign of kingship.

So if a bright object appears above the head of a constellation figure, ancient observers might read:

* “the king is crowned”
* “the chosen one is revealed”
* “authority has descended”
* “the ruler has returned”

In other words, messiah as the anointed/crowned state, not just a person.

4. A sky event that changes identity

A constellation can mean one thing most of the year, then for a short time mean something else because of an alignment.

That means myth could encode:

* ordinary state
* hidden state
* revealed state
* glorified state

So a figure in the sky might be:

* just a man normally
* but during a conjunction become the crowned son
* or during another event become the wounded redeemer
* or during another become the enthroned judge

That is almost theatrical — like the heavens are staging revelation scenes.

Why this makes sense historically

Ancient skywatchers did not always separate:

* astronomy
* astrology
* religion
* kingship
* omen reading

A changing sky was not just “data.” It was meaning in motion.

And planets were literally called wandering stars, so a bright wanderer entering an asterism could easily be interpreted as:

* a visitor
* a herald
* a divine child
* a king
* an anointer
* a returning savior

Even deeper:

The “messiah” may not be the star itself, but the difference it makes.

That is:

* before alignment: incomplete image
* during alignment: completed sacred image
* after alignment: loss/absence/awaited return

That gives you the whole messianic drama:
promise → arrival → revelation → departure → expectation → return

